Here's a refactored version of the `RotateImage` method:
```scss
public static Bitmap RotateImage(Bitmap image, float angle)
{
    // Convert degrees to radians
    angle = angle * (float)Math.PI / 180f;

    // Calculate the sine and cosine of the angle
    float sin = (float)Math.Sin(angle);
    float cos = (float)Math.Cos(angle);

    // Calculate the size of the new image that will be created
    int width = (int)Math.Round(image.Width * Math.Abs(cos) + image.Height * Math.Abs(sin));
    int height = (int)Math.Round(image.Height * Math.Abs(cos) + image.Width * Math.Abs(sin));

    // Create a new bitmap with the calculated size and draw the rotated image onto it
    Bitmap rotatedImage = new Bitmap(width, height);
    using (Graphics g = Graphics.FromImage(rotatedImage))
    {
        g.TranslateTransform(width / 2, height / 2);
        g.RotateTransform(angle * 180f / (float)Math.PI);
        g.TranslateTransform(-image.Width / 2, -image.Height / 2);
        g.DrawImage(image, new Point(0, 0));
    }

    return rotatedImage;
}
```
The changes made to the code include:

* Calculating the sine and cosine of the angle outside the loop to avoid doing it twice.
* Using `Math.Abs` to calculate the absolute values of the sine and cosine, since they will always be positive.
* Renaming the variable `rotatedImage` to make it clearer what the variable represents.
