Why can't I navigate back to the previous page with the browser back button if I use windows.replace
When you use the `window.location.replace()` method in JavaScript, it replaces the current page in the history stack with the new page, so when you press the browser's back button, it goes to the previous page before the current page instead of the page you replaced. This is because `window.location.replace()` method modifies the current page's URL without leaving a new page in the browser's history, so the browser doesn't have any page to go back to.
If you would like to keep the previous page in the history stack and able to go back to it, you can use `window.location.assign()` instead. This method will load the new page, but keep the current page in the history stack, so you can go back to it using the browser's back button.
