how do I clear sessionStorage when the tab is closed?
You can use the `window.onbeforeunload` event to clear the `sessionStorage` when the tab is closed. Here's an example of how you might do this:
```
window.onbeforeunload = function() {
    sessionStorage.clear();
};
```
This event is fired when the user is leaving the page (e.g. by closing the tab, navigating to another page, or refreshing the page). The `sessionStorage.clear()` method is used to remove all key/value pairs from the `sessionStorage` object.

You can also use `localStorage.clear()` to clear the `localStorage` when the tab is closed.

Please note that this event is not supported by all browsers and the behavior may vary across different browsers.
Do I insert it before or after document.ready
It depends on the specific code you are working with, but generally, it is best to put it before the `$(document).ready()` function, so that it is executed as soon as the page is loaded and not just when the DOM is ready. This is because the `window.onbeforeunload` event needs to be set up before the user closes the tab.
Can I make it clear the local storage only if the tab is closed and nothing else?
Yes, you can use the `onbeforeunload` event to detect when the tab is closed and clear the local storage in that event. You could also check the `event.clientY` property to make sure that the user is actually closing the tab and not just navigating to a different page on the same website.
```
window.onbeforeunload = function(event) {
    if (event.clientY < 0) {
        localStorage.clear();
    }
};
```
Note that onbeforeunload event is not a reliable way to detect tab closure as it is not supported by all browsers and can be blocked by the browser's pop-up blocker.
