```csharp
public bool HasPermission(string permission)
{
    // Check if the user is authenticated
    if (!User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
    {
        return false;
    }

    // Get the user's role(s)
    string[] roles = Roles.GetRolesForUser();

    // Check if any of the user's roles have the specified permission
    foreach (string role in roles)
    {
        if (Roles.IsUserInRole(role, permission))
        {
            return true;
        }
    }

    // If no role has the permission, return false
    return false;
}
```
In this example, the `HasPermission()` method checks if the current user is authenticated, gets the user's role(s) using the `Roles.GetRolesForUser()` method, and then checks if any of the user's roles have the specified permission using the `Roles.IsUserInRole()` method.

You can use this method to check if a user has permission to view a specific page or UI element in your ASP.NET web application. For example, you might call `HasPermission("page1_view")` to check if the current user has permission to view "page1".

